From: Rhnn Hur <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Subject: [PATCH] luci-app-airoha-npu: support AN7563, drop the overclock

AN7563 (AN7552 die, e.g. ipTIME BE5100M; OpenWrt subtarget
airoha/an7563). The package Makefile applies this patch only for
that subtarget, so the code assumes an AN7563 and does not detect the
SoC at run time.

- Never write registers. setOverclock and the raw PLL programming are
  removed, and the ACL no longer grants /dev/mem or cpufreq sysfs
  writes. CPU frequency changes only through cpufreq.
- On the AN7552 die the ATF owns the ARM PLL and caps it at 1000 MHz.
  setMaxFreq refuses anything above that or non-numeric, and the UI's
  Max Frequency list is filtered to match. setGovernor and setMaxFreq
  check that cpufreq is present.
- Frame Engine registers are read only from a fixed whitelist (PSE,
  GDM1/GDM2, CDM1/CDM2, PLL 0x1fa202ac/0x1fa202b8), and only while
  airoha_eth / airoha-eth is bound. Reading an unclocked block can hang
  the bus. The PSE walk covers 11 ports, like the stock fe_core.
- PLL readback uses the AN7552 decode: PCW 0x1fa202ac[31:24], POSDIV
  0x1fa202b8[20:18].
- The NPU firmware is looked up through the firmware-name of any npu@*
  node instead of the EN7581 npu@1e900000 only, with fallbacks.
- npu_cores no longer prints a second "0" when there is no NPU.
- JSON API: getStatus adds pll_valid and eth_bound. getFrameEngine
  drops gdm4 and returns {"error": ...} while the Ethernet driver is
  not bound.
- The UI hides GDM3 and GDM4, which the AN7552 does not have, labels
  GDM1 as the switch and GDM2 as the 2.5G WAN, and shows the two
  Wi-Fi bands of the MT7992.
- The Frame Engine NPU card shows the real core count (npu_cores) and
  that the AN7563 NPU firmware handles Wi-Fi RX (HW-RRO) only; the PPE
  is programmed by the host and Wi-Fi TX stays on host DMA/WED.

This patch is applied only when building for the airoha/an7563
subtarget (see the package Makefile).
